 ## Background
-SWAG was introduced as an extension to 3D Gaussian Splatting to address the challenge of transient occluders in casual photo captures [S1][S2, unofficial]. It builds on the principles of appearance conditioning and unsupervised learning to achieve robust static scene reconstruction [S1][S2, unofficial]. The method is part of the broader trend of improving 3DGS robustness in real-world scenarios [S1][S2, unofficial].
-
+Origin: SWAG was introduced in the paper "From Volume Rendering to 3D Gaussian Splatting: Theory and Applications" [S1][S2, unofficial].
+History and development: It extends 3DGS by conditioning Gaussians on learned appearance embeddings and training unsupervised transient Gaussians [S1][S2, unofficial].
+Where it is used: Used for static scene reconstruction in the presence of transient occluders [S1][S2, unofficial].
